  "worked_solution": "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n5(7)^{x}-35=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $35$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(7)^{x}-35 & =0 \\\\ 5(7)^{x}-\\cancel{35}+\\cancel{35} & =0+35 \\\\ 5(7)^{x} & =35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(7)^{x} & =35 \\\\ {7}^{x} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(7)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
